# AWS Credentials (one of the following methods)
# Option 1: Environment variables
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=your_access_key
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=your_secret_key
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-east-1

# Option 2: AWS profile (uses ~/.aws/credentials)
# AWS_PROFILE=my-profile

# Option 3: IAM role (no credentials needed if running on EC2/Lambda/ECS)

# Required IAM permissions (attach to your role/user):
# cloudwatch:GetMetricData
# cloudwatch:GetMetricStatistics
# lambda:ListFunctions
# dynamodb:ListTables
# dynamodb:DescribeTable
# sns:ListTopics
# sns:GetTopicAttributes
# s3:ListAllMyBuckets
# s3:GetBucketLocation
# events:ListRules
# logs:StartQuery
# logs:GetQueryResults
# logs:DescribeLogGroups

# ─── Azure Credentials ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Azure config is NOT read from this .env file or from process env vars.
# Copy .run-creds.sh.example to .run-creds.sh (gitignored) and fill in your
# values — CloudLens reads that file directly:
#   cp .run-creds.sh.example .run-creds.sh
#   node cloudlens.js --provider azure
#
# Use a different file with --azure-env-file <path>. Any --azure-subscription/
# --azure-tenant/--azure-client-id/--azure-client-secret/--azure-location CLI
# flag overrides the matching value from the file.
#
# If no client ID/secret are found (file or flags), CloudLens falls back to
# DefaultAzureCredential — supports `az login`, managed identity, etc.
#
# Required RBAC roles (assign at the subscription or resource-group scope):
# Reader              — list/read resource properties (VMs, storage accounts, Key Vaults,
#                        Service Bus namespaces, Event Grid/Hubs, API Management, CDN, etc.)
# Monitoring Reader    — read Azure Monitor metrics (CPU, request counts, RU consumption, etc.)
